<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- ADDED 17 Oct 2026 - track + arc as one progressDrawable.

     A layer-list rather than two sibling views, for two reasons. ProgressBar
     owns a single progressDrawable, so that is where the arc has to live; and
     L-076 permits only ONE ViewGroup per widget layout, so the ring cannot be
     assembled out of separate views either. One drawable is the only shape the
     constraint allows.

     THE TWO IDS ARE THE WHOLE DESIGN, and this file shipped without them first.
     ProgressBar addresses the layers of a layer-list BY ID:

       - setVisualProgress() looks up findDrawableByLayerId(R.id.progress) to
         decide which layer gets setLevel().
       - getTintTarget(R.id.progress, true) looks up the same id to decide which
         layer gets tinted.

     With no ids, BOTH lookups miss and fall back to the entire LayerDrawable.
     That fallback looks harmless and is not. LayerDrawable.onLevelChange
     forwards the level to EVERY child (LayerDrawable.java:1515-1533), and
     LayerDrawable.setTintList tints every child - so both layers receive the
     same level and the same urgency colour. Since both shapes declare useLevel
     and a ring draws a sweep of 360*level/10000 (GradientDrawable.java), the
     arc covers the track exactly: the widget rendered as ONE depleting arc with
     no muted remainder at all, i.e. the ring carried no more information than
     the number in its centre.

     So the track is @android:id/background and the arc is @android:id/progress.
     That is also what ProgressBar's own default horizontal drawable uses, and
     the reason ProgressBar's fallback comment says "the theme-default drawable
     will always have all layer IDs present".

     Order still matters: track first, arc second, so the arc draws over it.

     The -90 degree rotation is NOT here. It goes on the ProgressBar as
     android:rotation="-90", because rotating the whole view moves the sweep's
     start from 3 o'clock to 12 o'clock while leaving the full-circle track
     looking identical either way. Rotating the arc layer in isolation instead
     needs negative insets to undo the layout offset, which is a hack whose
     behaviour depends on the bounds the launcher hands the drawable. -->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item
        android:id="@android:id/background"
        android:drawable="@drawable/widget_ring_track" />
    <item
        android:id="@android:id/progress"
        android:drawable="@drawable/widget_ring_arc" />
</layer-list>